A1. An inspiration of song-writers. ~ MOTHER
A7. Where is the perfect man? ~ UNBORN
A12. This means "it won't be long now." ~ SOON
A13. The first thing a man lost for a woman. ~ RIB
A15. What Sugar Daddy is to the gold-digger. ~ MINE
A17. Part of the verb "to be." ~ IS
A18. This kind of labor often consists in making little ones out of big ones. ~ PENAL
A19. What Columbus was looking for. ~ EI
A21. By-product raised by hay-makers. ~ WELTS
A23. What Tunney and Heeney did last year. ~ MET
A24. Jumpers. ~ FROGS
A26. What examinations require. (Abbr.) ~ ANS
A27. This is a relief to the dry farmer. ~ CIDER
A29. Three letters which, pronounced aloud, sound like the first name of an English King whose wives quite lost their heads about him. ~ NRE
A30. Where some people get into ruts. (Abbr.) ~ RD
A31. What reformers seek and find. ~ DIRT
A33. These are subjects for great undertakings. ~ DEAD
A36. Beginning of the answer to an embarrassing question. ~ ER
A37. Her exploits were reel thrillers. ~ ELAINE
A40. These make away with a lot of liquid. ~ DRAINS
A42. A tree. ~ ELM
A43. Famous old salts from Surrey. ~ EPSOM
A44. This makes a warm neck. ~ BOA
A45. There are still bass innuendos about brave hearts, the ocean, and this State. ~ ASLEEP
A47. This is too much to do. ~ OVERDO
A50. Either half of the name of an Egyptian goddess. ~ IS
A51. What Ulysses did when he met the suitors. ~ SLEW
A53. In the head this brings contentment, apparently; in the stomach, distress. ~ VOID
A54. In the same place. (Abbr.) ~ IB
A55. A warning from the car ahead. ~ SLO
A57. The ancient conqueror. ~ ROMAN
A59. One (Scotch). ~ ANE
A60. What the new horseback rider must have been doing when he said he wouldn't stand for such treatment. ~ LYING
A62. Take this if you want to see the world. ~ MAP
A63. These are very low places. ~ DIVES
A65. A French article. ~ LE
A66. The fiddler's friend. ~ RESIN
A67. An infantile monosyllable usually applied to Daddy. ~ DA
A68. What Miss Shorthorn and the Jersey's daughter are—under the skin. ~ VEAL
A70. The preacher is an optimist who takes this to mean assent. ~ NOD
A71. How the ball player got home. ~ SLID
A73. Some women have waistlines—others have these. ~ GIRTHS
A74. You can always find these around the big guns. ~ SHELLS

D2. Principal part of many heads. (Latin.) ~ OS
D3. What a relief when the law has the villain in these. ~ TOILS
D4. One man who is always at the service of his party. ~ HOST
D5. The printer's gift to cross-words. ~ EN
D6. What Juliet did for Romeo. ~ PINED
D8. A border State. (Abbr.) ~ NM
D9. This carries one to the final destination. ~ BIER
D10. What every farmer should know. ~ ONION
D11. Second word in body of business letter. ~ RE
D13. A word that very, very frequently follows the word "please." ~ REMIT
D14. You aren't a real fisherman unless your breath is this way too. ~ BATED
D16. Conscious. ~ AWARE
D20. If it wasn't for these you wouldn't have been stuck for that used car. ~ USERS
D22. When it comes to riding a hobby, how do the women go on? ~ ENDLESSLY
D25. A household flavoring extract on the lips of the nation since Judge Jr. has shown How. ~ GRENADINE
D27. You've got to be this before you can stand on your own two feet. ~ CREEPER
D28. Some drink it; some gaze at it—the combination is worse. (Two words.) ~ REDMOON
D31. Rockefeller says to hang on to these. ~ DIMES
D32. A good place to be when it rains. ~ IN
D34. Abbreviation used in that great Railway Classic. ~ AR
D35. What a night worker sleeps in. ~ DABED
D38. The entire. ~ ALL
D39. A silly beast. ~ ASS
D41. Suffix used to form the comparative degree. ~ IOR
D45. This leads to the altar. ~ AISLE
D46. A Spanish article. ~ EL
D48. A nickname for Violet. ~ VI
D49. Those who cure these, live off the fat of the land. ~ OBESE
D52. Kipling says these are deadly. ~ WOMEN
D53. What life would be without Vertical 52. ~ VAPID
D56. This fellow deals in smooth stuff. ~ OILER
D58. Member of a secret order. ~ MASON
D59. Be of value or profit. ~ AVAIL
D61. Even the unkempt like their liquor this way. ~ NEAT
D64. You'll never know these by their works. ~ IDLE
D68. Same as Vertical 48. ~ VI
D69. One-half on the team. (Abbr.) ~ LH
D71. This means "speak easy." ~ SH
D72. Dolorous Lilies. (Abbr.) ~ DL


